{
  "summary": "This study investigates how ultrathin oxide memristors achieve near-ideal area-type switching by suppressing parasitic charge effects. Using TiN/Ta2O5/Pt layers (3-10 nm), researchers identified a 5-6 nm thickness window where tunneling-dominated transport minimizes readout drift and non-pinched hysteresis while maintaining analog switching. The findings reveal trap-assisted tunneling governs stabilized states, and ionic redistribution affects state reconstruction, enabling robust multi-state memristors for in-memory computing.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Ultrathin TiN/Ta2O5/Pt layers (5-6 nm) suppress non-pinched hysteresis and short-time drift",
    "Trap-assisted tunneling dominates stabilized-state response in near-ideal switching",
    "Ionic redistribution governs pristine-state reconstruction and stability boundaries"
  ],
  "study_type": "Device characterization and modeling",
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes ultrathin scaling as a fundamental design principle for achieving near-ideal area-type memristors, enabling robust multi-state operation with minimal parasitic effects for in-memory computing applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study used spatial lipidomics and transcriptomics to investigate lipid composition in human articular cartilage across healthy and diseased states. It revealed layer-dependent lipid distributions, with surface regions enriched in phospholipids and subchondral regions enriched in lyso-phospholipids, correlating with region-specific gene expression. The findings suggest ceramide's role in chondrocyte function and potential protective effects in early osteoarthritis.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Surface cartilage layers show phospholipid enrichment linked to matrix secretion genes",
    "Subchondral regions feature lyso-ph:lipids associated with biomineralization genes",
    "Ceramide mediates chondrocyte secretion and may protect against early osteoarthritis"
  ],
  "study_type": "Spatial lipidomic and transcriptomic analysis",
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes a spatial lipid architecture in cartilage that correlates with functional gene expression, providing a material basis for biomimetic cartilage engineering strategies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the demographic impacts of Roman colonization and early medieval migrations on the Iberian Peninsula using ancient DNA from 243 individuals spanning 100-80. Genetic analysis reveals significant Eastern Mediterranean and North African ancestry during Roman rule, followed by Germanic and Alan influences post-5th century. The Hispano-Roman population maintained continuity despite political shifts, contrasting with Britain and the central Mediterranean where demographic changes were more pronounced.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Roman period: influx of Eastern Mediterranean and North African ancestry, particularly in central and southern Iberia",
    "Early medieval period: Germanic and Alan (Sarmatian) groups gained political control starting in the 5th century",
    "Hispano-Roman population maintained demographic continuity across Iberia despite political transitions"
  ],
  "study_type": "Ancient DNA analysis",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings demonstrate regional variation in demographic continuity and admixture patterns across post-Roman Europe, highlighting how political transitions affected population structure differently than in other parts of Europe."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates enhancing high-voltage lithium-metal batteries' temperature resilience by designing an electrolyte with solvation-equilibrium locking. Using dimethyl sulfite and Pyr13DFOB, the researchers created an electrolyte that maintains stable ion solvation structures from -60°C to 60°C, preventing thermal degradation. The resulting batteries operate effectively at extreme temperatures, including -85°C and 120°C, with high energy density under lean conditions.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Solvation-equilibrium-locked electrolyte maintains stable solvent-separated ion pairs and contact ion pairs across a wide temperature range (-60°C to 60°C).",
    "The electrolyte reduces solvent coordination around Li+, minimizing interfacial decomposition and gas evolution at extreme temperatures.",
    "Li||NMC811 cells achieve discharge capacity at -85°C and operate up to 120°C, with pouch cells remaining functional down to -122.9°C."
  ],
  "study_type": "Electrolyte design with molecular dynamics simulations and in situ NMR/Raman analysis",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a molecular design strategy for lithium-metal batteries that enables ultrawide-temperature operation without compromising electrochemical stability, advancing the practical deployment of high-energy batteries in extreme environments."
}

{
  "summary": "This study engineered mouse mast cells to express cytotoxic proteins (perforin and granzyme B) for targeted cancer cell killing via IgE-mediated recognition. In a melanoma model, these cells selectively lysed OVA-expressing cancer cells, induced immune remodeling with increased lymphocyte infiltration, and synergistically inhibited tumor growth when combined with 5-fluorouracil, reducing myeloid-derived suppressor cells.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Genetically engineered mast cells overexpress perforin and granzyme B for targeted cancer cell cytotoxicity via IgE-antigen recognition",
    "PG-MCs induced immune remodeling with increased lymphocyte infiltration in the tumor microenvironment",
    "Combination with 5-fluorouracil significantly inhibited tumor growth by depleting myeloid-derived suppressor cells"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo mouse model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a strategy for engineering mast cells as targeted cytotoxic effectors that can modulate the tumor microenvironment through IgE-mediated mechanisms, offering a potential platform for immunotherapy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated proteomic changes in T cell exhaustion using multi-omic integration. By combining transcriptomics, quantitative proteomics, turnover proteomics, and ubiquitin proteomics, it identified pathways regulated by transcriptional and post-translational mechanisms, including ubiquitin modifications. Key findings include widespread proteostasis collapse, shifts in the ubiquitin-proteasome system, and E3 ligase RNF1. The results highlight proteostatic collapse as central to T cell exhaustion and suggest targeting E3 ligases to improve immunotherapy outcomes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Widespread proteostasis collapse in T cell exhaustion",
    "Differential expression of E3 ubiquitin ligases correlates with immunotherapy outcomes",
    "Overexpression of RNF166 enhances T cell persistence in vitro and in vivo"
  ],
  "study_type": "Multi-omic integration of transcriptomics, quantitative proteomics, turnover proteomics, and ubiquitin proteomics",
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes proteostatic collapse as a central mechanism in T cell exhaustion, providing a foundation for targeting E3 ligases to improve cancer immunotherapy efficacy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how dual twisted interfaces in CVD-grown trilayer MoS2 affect second harmonic generation (SHG). By fabricating nine distinct stacking configurations, the researchers analyzed Raman and photoluminescence data to show interface-dependent phonon and excitonic responses. Atomic-resolution imaging confirmed dual twisted interfaces and a supermoiré lattice. They developed a framework linking dual interface geometry to SHG intensity and polarization, demonstrating TT-MoS2's potential for tunable nonlinear optical engineering.",
  "key_findings": [
    "CVD-grown trilayer MoS2 with dual twisted interfaces exhibits stacking-dependent phonon and excitonic responses",
    "Atomic-resolution techniques confirmed dual twisted interfaces and supermoiré lattice formation",
    "A framework linking dual interface geometry to SHG intensity and polarization was established"
  ],
  "study_type": "Chemical vapor deposition (CVD) grown trilayer MoS2 with nine distinct stacking configurations",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a foundational framework for engineering tunable nonlinear optical responses in van der Waals materials through multi-interface twisted stacking."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses deep-tumor photodynamic therapy limitations by designing a self-assembling prodrug that stores singlet oxygen and suppresses nitric oxide-mediated resistance. The nano-prodrug co-assembles with protoporphyrin IX, releases singlet oxygen upon light activation, and uses glutathione-responsive disulfide bonds to target tumor microenvironments. It demonstrates potent tumor suppression in pancreatic models with minimal toxicity, offering a strategy to overcome light penetration and hypoxia barriers.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Self-assembling nano-prodrug co-assembles with protoporphyrin IX for targeted singlet oxygen delivery",
    "Glutathione-responsive disulfide bonds enable tumor-specific singlet oxygen release",
    "iNOS inhibition reduces nitric oxide overproduction, enhancing singlet oxygen efficacy"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo tumor model",
  "scientific_significance": "This approach provides a light-independent mechanism to overcome physical and adaptive resistance barriers in deep-tissue photodynamic therapy by synchronizing singlet oxygen release with tumor microenvironment dynamics."
}

{
  "summary": "This study developed KY0418, a bioavailable covalent GPX4 inhibitor, to induce ferroptosis in vivo. The compound demonstrated selective targeting of GPX4SeCys46, triggered ferroptotic changes in cancer models, and suppressed metastasis while eradicating circulating tumor cells across multiple cancer types. Its efficacy varied by tumor type but consistently inhibited metastasis.",
  "key_findings": [
    "KY0418 selectively inhibits GPX4SeCys46 to induce ferroptosis in human and mouse cancer cells",
    "KY0418 triggers ferroptotic ultrastructural changes and hydroxyl radical production in vivo",
    "KY0418 consistently eradicates circulating tumor cells and suppresses metastasis across multiple cancer models"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo mouse model with acute-liver-injury and tumor models",
  "scientific_significance": "KY0418 provides a bioavailable tool for studying ferroptosis in vivo, advancing therapeutic translation of ferroptosis-inducing strategies for cancer."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how structural anisotropy in electrolytes improves ion transport for ionic actuators. By fabricating anisotropic electrolytes with oriented channels via directional freeze-drying, the researchers achieved twice the through-plane ion mobility compared to disordered electrolytes. This enhanced ion transport led to better actuator performance while balancing strain and blocking force, with effects observed across multiple polymer systems.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Anisotropic electrolytes fabricated via directional freeze-drying exhibit twice the through-plane ion mobility of disordered electrolytes",
    "Directional confinement significantly enhances ionic act. performance by mitigating strain-blocking force trade-offs",
    "The effect is reproducible across multiple polymer systems, underscoring structural anisotropy's role in ion transport regulation"
  ],
  "study_type": "Fabrication of anisotropic electrolytes with oriented channels via directional freeze-drying",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes structural anisotropy as a critical design principle for optimizing ion transport in electrolytes, advancing the understanding of how microstructural features govern electrochemical actuator performance beyond conventional random porous systems."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated RE-free magnesium-zinc alloys for biodegradable stents, comparing MZ01 and ZM115 against WE43. Using electrochemical analyses and in vivo models, it found MZ01 exhibited superior corrosion resistance, uniform degradation, reduced microcracks, and better vascular healing with lower inflammation and neointimal thickness compared to RE-containing stents. The alloy composition directly influenced microstructure and biocompatibility, supporting RE-free stents as viable cardiovascular scaffolds.",
  "key_findings": [
    "MZ01 demonstrated higher charge-transfer resistance and uniform degradation in mice",
    "MZ01 showed reduced microcrack area and attenuated inflammatory responses at Day 56",
    "MZ01 exhibited lower neointimal thickness and enhanced endothelialization in rabbit arteries"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo animal models and electrochemical analyses",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a composition-microstructure-corrosion-cellular response axis for RE-free magnesium-zinc alloys, providing a pathway to biodegradable stents with controlled degradation and improved biocompatibility for cardiovascular applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates using engineered non-LTR retrotransposons for efficient in vivo gene delivery. The researchers developed Gene Writers that integrate reporter genes and CAR-T cells ex vivo with antitumor activity. In vivo applications in humanized mice showed stable B cell depletion via lipid nanoparticle RNA delivery, demonstrating a framework for gene-sized cargo delivery.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Engineered non-LTR retrotransposons (Gene Writers) enable efficient reporter gene insertion in human cells",
    "Gene Writers generated functional CAR-T cells with robust antitumor activity ex vivo",
    "In vivo CAR-T cell generation and B cell depletion achieved in humanized mice via lipid nanoparticle RNA delivery"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo studies using humanized mouse models",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes non-LTR retrotransposons as a viable platform for precise, large-scale gene delivery in vivo, addressing a critical limitation in current gene therapy approaches."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the integration of layered spin-crossover metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with two-dimensional quantum materials to achieve light-induced control over their electronic and magnetic properties. Using thermal and light-triggered spin transitions, the researchers demonstrated selective modulation of conductivity in graphene, magnetic hysteresis in CrSBr bilayers, and superconducting critical current modulation in NbSe2. The findings establish these SCO MOFs as switchable building blocks for strain-mediated control in multifunctional electronics.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Light-induced switching of conductivity in graphene",
    "MOF-triggered magnetic hysteresis in CrSBr bilayers",
    "Light modulation of superconducting critical current in NbSe2"
  ],
  "study_type": "Molecular approach with layered spin-crossover MOFs",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a molecular strategy for strain-mediated control of electronic, magnetic, and superconducting functionalities in 2D quantum materials using stimuli-responsive spin-crossover frameworks, expanding MOF applications beyond traditional porous systems."
}

{
  "summary": "This study engineered a thermosensitive asparaginase fusion protein (ASNase-ELP-LHRH) to enhance tumor-targeted metabolic therapy. By integrating elastin-like polypeptide (ELP) for prolonged half-life and luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone (LHRH) for tumor-specific delivery, the construct achieved sustained asparagine depletion, inhibited metastasis via epithelial-mesenchymal transition suppression, and synergized with immunotherapy to extend survival in triple-negative breast cancer models.",
  "key_findings": [
    "ELP extension increased asparaginase half-life to ∼400 hours (95-fold) and reduced immunogenicity 70-fold",
    "LHRH-mediated targeting suppressed metastasis by downregulating Twist1 and upregulating E-cadherin",
    "Combination with anti-PD-L1 achieved durable metastasis inhibition for 40 days and 1.5-fold survival extension"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo models (triple-negative breast cancer)",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a modular protein engineering strategy to overcome metabolic therapy limitations in solid tumors, enabling synergistic metabolic-immunotherapy for sustained antitumor effects."
}

{
  "summary": "This study identifies MCT6 as a key intestinal transporter for the metabolite N-lactoyl-phenylalanine (Lac-Phe), which regulates metformin-induced weight loss. Using genetic knockout models in mice, the research demonstrates that MCT6 is essential for Lac-Phe efflux from the gut, maintaining systemic Lac-Phe levels during metformin treatment. Loss of MCT6 blocks metformin's weight loss effect, while exogenous Lac-Phe rescues this phenotype, highlighting a critical link between gut metabolism and systemic metabolic responses.",
  "key_findings": [
    "MCT6 is an intestinal transporter specifically required for Lac-Phe efflux",
    "MCT6 deletion in mice prevents metformin-induced weight loss",
    "Exogenous Lac-Phe restores body weight in MCT6-knockout mice"
  ],
  "study_type": "Mouse genetic knockout models",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a direct mechanism by which an intracellular metabolite is exported via a specific transporter to exert systemic metabolic effects, providing a model for understanding how gut-derived metabolites influence whole-body physiology."
}

{
  "summary": "This perspective article addresses the reproducibility crisis in metal-bacteria interaction research by identifying key sources of inconsistency and proposing a roadmap for mechanistic studies. It emphasizes the need for causal perturbations, orthogonal measurements, and cross-laboratory validation to improve reproducibility in this field.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The reproducibility crisis in metal-bacteria interaction studies stems from methodological inconsistencies",
    "A source-specific experimental roadmap is provided for mechanistic investigations",
    "Cross-laboratory validation and causal perturbations are critical for improving reproducibility"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a framework for mechanistic investigations in metal-bacteria interactions, advancing the field toward more reproducible and causal understanding of antibacterial metal mechanisms."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines barriers to translating biomaterial-based cytokine therapies for wound healing, focusing on release kinetics and design parameters of hydrogels, particles, and scaffolds. It identifies clinical trial challenges since 2.012 and highlights gaps in translating preclinical findings to clinical use despite existing FDA-approved cytokine therapies.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Limited clinical adoption of biomaterial-based cytokine therapies for wound healing despite preclinical advances",
    "Becaplermin remains the only FDA-approved cytokine therapy for wound healing since 1997",
    "Critical gaps exist in translating hydrogel, particle, and scaffold designs to clinical applications"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This review identifies persistent translational barriers in cytokine delivery systems for wound healing, emphasizing the need for optimized release kinetics and design parameters to advance clinical implementation of biomaterial-based therapies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how lagging-strand DNA synthesis is coordinated with leading-strand synthesis in eukaryotes using single-molecule imaging in Xenopus extracts. It found that neither Pol α nor Pol δ is stably tethered to the replisome, revealing distributive lagging-strand synthesis where new polymerase molecules are rapidly recruited per Okazaki fragment. This indicates leading- and lagging-strand synthesis are not intrinsically coupled, resolving the topological challenge through dynamic polymerase recruitment.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Lagging-strand synthesis occurs distributively with rapid recruitment of Pol α and Pol δ for each Okazaki fragment",
    "Neither Pol α nor Pol δ is stably tethered to the replisome",
    "Leading- and lagging-strand synthesis are not intrinsically coupled in metazoans"
  ],
  "study_type": "Single-molecule imaging in Xenopus nuclear extracts",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings demonstrate that metazoans resolve anti-parallel DNA replication topology through dynamic polymerase recruitment rather than intrinsic coupling of leading- and lagging-strand synthesis."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of achieving low threshold voltage (Vth) and high performance in n-type organic electrochemical transistors (OECTs) by developing a quinoxaline-based polymeric semiconductor via stepwise cyanation. The modified material QxCN-CNTVT exhibits a deep LUMO level (-4.23 eV), resulting in a low Vth of 0.13 V and high µC* value (168.89 F cm-1 V-1 s-1), with stable operation and successful application in EOG/ECG signal amplification and logic gates.",
  "key_findings": [
    "QxCN-CNTVT achieves a low threshold voltage of 0.13 V and high µC* value of 168.89 F cm-1 V-1 s-1",
    "The material demonstrates superior operational stability, retaining ∼80% of initial current after 10,000 s of continuous operation",
    "QxCN-CNTVT enables efficient amplification of EOG/ECG signals and integrated logic gates"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a molecular design strategy for n-type OECTs that simultaneously achieves low threshold voltage and high performance, advancing the field of low-power bioelectronic devices."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how spontaneous phase separation in Janus hydrogels can spatiotemporally orchestrate fibrotic progression for functional tendon regeneration. The researchers engineered a Janus hydrogel (SPS-J. The hydrogel features an outer methacrylated hyaluronic acid layer for anti-adhesion and an inner PLD layer for mechanical reinforcement. It releases COX-2 siRNA early to suppress fibroblast hyperactivation and niclosamide later to polarize macrophages and suppress fibrogenesis. In rat models, it reduced adhesions and promoted functional regeneration with coordinated mechanotransduction and tissue remodeling.",
  "key_findings": [
    "SPS-JH reduces peritendinous adhesions in rat Achilles tendon injury models",
    "SPS-JH releases COX-2 siRNA to silence fibroblast hyperactivation during early inflammation",
    "SPS-JH sustains niclosamide release to polarize macrophages and suppress fibrogenesis during proliferation"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo rat model study",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a structurally minimalist hydrogel design that uses spontaneous phase separation to dynamically coordinate fibrotic progression, providing a template for spatiotemporally controlled tissue regeneration."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how lifestyle factors induce regulatory T cell fragility leading to immune-related adverse events (irAEs) during immune checkpoint blockade. Using a mouse model (LISA) that mimics human susceptibility through diet-driven metabolic and microbial changes, the research demonstrates that dysbiosis, particularly Akkerm. muciniphila enrichment, causes Treg instability and inflammation. Interventions like dietary changes and IFN-γ blockade resolved irAEs, highlighting dietary and microbial factors as modifiable targets to reduce immunotherapy toxicity.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Diet-driven metabolic and microbial alterations in the LISA model recapitulate multi-organ irAEs with immune infiltration and cytokine production.",
    "Akkermansia muciniphila enrichment correlates with regulatory T cell fragility and CD8+ cytotoxic effector expansion.",
    "Interventions including dietary reversal, metabolite supplementation, and IFN-γ blockade restored Treg stability and resolved inflammation."
  ],
  "study_type": "Mouse model (LISA)",
  "scientific_significance": "The study identifies dietary and microbial factors as modifiable targets to mitigate immune-related adverse events during immunotherapy, providing a mechanistic basis for improving therapeutic safety without compromising efficacy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates a switchable liquid-bridge adhesion mechanism for robotic pick-and-release operations. Using a two-tiered stamp with spatially patterned wettability, the researchers guide liquid spreading and retraction to enable conformal contact with rough surfaces and damage-free manipulation of fragile microstructures. The approach achieves preload-free adhesion switching, tunable forces, and robust performance across varied surface conditions.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Boundary-guided pinning confines liquid spreading to form stable bridges for pickup",
    "Guided liquid retraction enables controlled release without structural damage",
    "The strategy supports robust manipulation across surfaces with diverse roughness and wusing"
  ],
  "study_type": "Experimental",
  "scientific_significance": "This work converts liquid-bridge adhesion from an uncontrolled capillary phenomenon into a controllable interfacial mechanism, enabling ultra-gentle robotic manipulation for microelectronic assembly applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study developed a biomimetic bimetallic nanoplatform (MFHM) combining pH-responsive manganese-iron layered double hydroxide with macrophage membrane to target biofilm infections. The platform disrupts bacterial biofilms via redox cycling and reactive oxygen species, while enhancing cGAS-STING and NF-κB signaling to stimulate coordinated innate and adaptive immune responses, reducing bacterial burden in methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus infections without systemic toxicity.",
  "key_findings": [
    "MFHM disrupts bacterial biofilms through Fe/Mn redox cycling and reactive oxygen species generation",
    "MFHM enhances cGAS-ST.ING and NF-κB signaling to promote pro-inflammatory macrophage polarization and phagocytosis",
    "MFHM reduces bacterial burden in methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus implant infections without systemic toxicity"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a biomimetic catalytic-immune framework for targeting biofilm infections through dual mechanisms of biofilm disruption and immune activation, offering a novel therapeutic strategy for recalcitrant infections."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates taurine's role in aging-related inflammation by examining its impact on NLRP3 inflammasome activation in macrophages. In aged mice, elevated taurine improved health span and reduced mortality from sepsis and inflammasome-mediated inflammation. Mechanistically, taurine efflux from macrophages is triggered by NLRP3 activators, and restoring taurine suppresses inflammasome activation. Human data shows caloric restriction increases adipose taurine and lowers inflammation, suggesting taurine acts as an immunometabolic rheostat to regulate inflammaging.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Elevated taurine in aged mice enhances health span and reduces mortality from sepsis and inflammasome-mediated inflammation",
    "Taurine efflux from macrophages is triggered by NLRP3 activators, leading to inflammasome activation",
    "Restoring taurine in macrophages suppresses NLRP3 inflammasome activation and prevents trans-Golgi network disassembly"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo mouse models and human adipose tissue analysis",
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies taurine as a key immunometabolic regulator that suppresses NLRP3 inflammasome activation, providing a mechanistic basis for mitigating age-related inflammation and potential therapeutic strategies for inflammasomopathies."
}
